  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThe main upper trough continues to rotate to our north over\ncentral Canada, with cooler air aloft allowing for a fair amount\nof cumulus to develop over the region this afternoon. The cloud\ncover will dissipate after sunset, leaving surface high \npressure and dew points in the 40s. At this point think we \nshould stay above the 40 mark overnight, but a few spots could \nget into the low to mid 40s. A couple of the model runs have \nsome light precip along the Canadian border tomorrow as a \nreinforcing shortwave rounds the base of the upper trough, but \nNBM probs of anything measurable are less than 10 percent. A \nstronger shortwave moving through could bring a few showers \nThursday afternoon and evening, but main impacts look to be \nbrief downpours and some lightning as surface based CAPE looks \npretty limited and should remain under 1000 J/kg. Slightly \ncooler and quiet on Friday as a cold front and then high \npressure come down behind the shortwave. \n\nEnsembles are in pretty good agreement on northwesterly flow\naloft continuing through the weekend and into the start of next\nweek, with various weak shortwaves moving through. NBM has keyed\nin on one of those shortwaves coming through Sunday night and\ninto Monday bringing fairly high probabilities of at least 0.01\nof precip. Given uncertainty with the timing of such features in\nthe long term see little reason to change it at this point.\nTemperatures seem fairly seasonable with highs in the upper 70s\nto low 80s and lows in the 50s.",
